diff --git a/docs/functionality.md b/docs/functionality.md
index 83d6bfc..aa15602 100644
--- a/docs/functionality.md
+++ b/docs/functionality.md
@@ -2,6 +2,8 @@
 
 The supervisor is a local MCP coordinator for one-shot Mistral Vibe 2.26.1 programmatic runs through the enforced `--legacy-harness` path. Its tools are `vibe_review_start`, `vibe_edit_start`, `vibe_status`, `vibe_result`, and `vibe_close`. A run accepts one task and fixed turn, deadline, event, transcript, and artifact limits. There is no interactive permission response, continuation, queue, ACP session, idle-session retention, or automatic recovery.
 
+The pinned runtime checks the effective local Vibe tool inventory against the exact run profile and fails closed if a required tool is missing or an unexpected tool is present. When `VIBE_SUPERVISOR_DIAGNOSTICS=1` is present in the supervisor process environment, private worker diagnostics also include that inventory and bounded tool invocations that reach `BaseTool.invoke`. They record approved built-in tool names and `attempt`, `completed`, or `failed` only; unrecognized names collapse to `<other>`, and arguments, paths, results, provider traffic, and reasoning are excluded. They do not capture requests rejected before invocation, so missing calls do not establish that no attempt or denial occurred. The diagnostics file is returned as an optional artifact when it is a regular file and fits the run's artifact limit. The hooks are pinned to inspected Vibe 2.26.1 source and signatures and fail closed on drift. Their absence means diagnostics were disabled or unavailable; neither readiness nor this local trace proves hosted model usability.
+
 The capacity guarantee belongs to one owning server/storage instance. The server reserves its single slot before asynchronous preparation and rejects another start with a capacity error; there is no waiting record or hidden worker. Separate isolated MCP connections can each own an instance. A second process cannot manage the same storage while the owner lock is held.
 
 ## Readiness and admission
